    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, i'm working on a doc for our new CI/CD pipeline. can u give me a quick breakdown of the main stages—code commit, automated testing, staging, and production—and specifically what kind of gates or checks we should have at each step to make sure nothing breaks?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "A CI/CD pipeline flows through stages like a well-organized line of quality control for your code. At the **code commit** stage, you need gates like linting and unit tests to catch immediate errors. Before moving to **automated testing**, ensure integration tests run against a dedicated environment to check component interactions. **Staging** requires end-to-end tests and perhaps performance checks, while **production** deployment should have final approval gates, like manual sign-off or canary releases. These gates act as checkpoints, preventing faulty code from reaching the end-user.",
      "source": "target",
